  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Friday)\nIssued at 1255 PM MST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nThe next system of note is forecast to arrive Monday afternoon. In a \nsomewhat similar fashion to today's storm, it is forecast to deepen \nas a trough as it moves into our state. This looks to bring \nscattered rain and snow showers across the forecast area, starting \nacross the northern mountains Monday afternoon, before pushing to \neastern NM through Monday night/Tuesday morning. There are a few \ndifferences between model runs on how the system progresses through \nthe state. The differences primarily stem from how deep the through \ngets as it moves over us, and the strength of the high pressure \nacross the central Great Plains (which affects a stationary boundary \nin eastern NM which could act as a source of isentropic lift). A \nbulk of models favor a less deep trough and a stronger boundary, \nleading to a larger coverage of precipitation at the moment. This \nlooks to squeeze out another inch or two on the northern mountains, \nand with some locations in eastern NM seeing their first snowflakes. \nWill need to keep an eye on model trends through the next several \nruns.\n\nRidging mixed with northwest flow looks to set in midweek, bringing \na break in active weather for the state. Temperatures look to \nrespond accordingly, raising a few degrees, but remaining primarily \nbelow average. The next system of note looks to arrive to the Desert \nSouthwest by Thursday, however models are not showing much in the \nway of precipitation at the current moment. There is significant \nspread in whether or not this feature becomes a closed low, and how \nfar it traverses southward into Mexico. The current outlook is that, \nat most, this system misses our forecast area with precipitation, \nwith not much in the way of sensible weather impacts for \nThanksgiving. Forecast confidence significantly decreases past \nThanksgiving.&&",
